<ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">party</ansi>

A player party coordinates companies. Each owner keeps authority over their
own companions, formation, equipment and retreat. An invitation is not membership.

  party create                    Start a party you lead.
  party invite [name]             Invite a player.
  party accept / party decline    Answer an invitation.
  party list                      See members, leader and your consent.
  party leave                     Leave; another member becomes leader.
  party disband                   Disband a party you lead.
  party kick [name]               Remove a member (leader only).
  party promote [name]            Transfer leadership (leader only).
  party chat [message]            Speak to accepted members.
  party autoattack on / off       Join your leader's mob attacks automatically.
  party follow on / off           Follow your current leader on ordinary exits.
  party support on / off          Consent to allied group helpful effects.

All three consents default off. Leaving or being kicked clears them. A new
leader clears following and autoattack for everyone: opt in again if desired.
Following queues your own movement command, with your own movement checks.
It does not start shared journeys or camps, and cannot move a distant company.
A separated follower receives a notice when the leader takes an ordinary exit.

Allied/area support requires both owners to turn support on and be present.
A chant loses targets if consent or membership is revoked. Company-scoped
spells, including Minor Heal All, still help only the caster's own company.
Single-target support can name a patient beside you outside a battle. In
a shared battle, cross-company support requires mutual consent and both
companies fighting a common living enemy; unrelated battles block support.

Accepted membership and leadership survive logout, restart and copyover.
Invitations and all three consents reset on recovery; logout clears your consent.
A departing leader is succeeded by an online member when there is one.
Each company saves its own roster and assets. A failed alliance save
cancels the membership change.

Each enemy pays one XP pool, split equally among living, connected company
owners beside it who dealt positive damage and still fight that enemy. An
invitation, idle membership or damage to a different enemy earns no share.
Withdrawn, distant and fallen owners receive none. Living companions still
attached in the room receive their owner's share. Integer leftovers go to
ascending player IDs. Elite XP bonuses apply once before splitting.

When several companies qualify, the enemy's carried loot and gold go into
its corpse, claimed by one of them; only the claimant may take it (get all
corpse), then give items to allies. If every qualifying owner is in one
party, the claim rotates among them from enemy to enemy. If not, it goes to
whoever dealt the enemy the most damage. Gear still worn by the body stays
on it. Nobody else may bury a corpse holding claimed loot. The claim ends
when the corpse crumbles (about 2 1/2 minutes); loot left in it is lost.
A kill pays only once.
Battle and corpse state are temporary and are not replayed after restart.
Mercy retains one settlement owner and its existing durable recovery rules.

Legacy party ranks are display labels only. Use formation for your company's
3x3 positions; allies may use the same cells in their own independent grids.

A random encounter belongs to the company it sprang on: only that company and
its party or alliance may fight it or draw its attention, and each allied
company rolls its own spoils (<ansi fg="command">help encounters</ansi>).

See <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help friendly-effects</ansi>, and <ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>.
